# Per-package statement-coverage floor, in percent.
#
# F-23 (docs/security-hardening.md §3): three of the packages that handle
# untrusted input shipped at 0.0 % coverage. This file is the ratchet -- CI
# fails when a package drops below its recorded floor, so coverage can only go
# up.
#
# The numbers are MEASURED, not aspirational. A floor of 0 is not a floor: it
# certifies nothing, and a package recorded at 0 can lose every test it has and
# still pass. Each value below was taken with the command CI runs
#
#   go test -race -covermode=atomic ./...
#
# on the harden/security-overhaul-v1 branch, then rounded DOWN by two to three
# points. The margin absorbs the only legitimate source of drift -- a test whose
# path depends on timing under -race -- and nothing else: a real regression is
# tens of points, not two. Measured at the time of writing: audit 62.9,
# auth/basic 86.5, auth/jwt 90.3, auth/oidc 94.1, auth/totp 86.2,
# auth/webauthn 85.9, middleware 69.3, provider 92.9, session 88.2,
# storage 97.9.
#
# Raising a floor after new tests land is a one-line change here. LOWERING one
# is a decision to accept a regression, and belongs in a review, not in a commit
# that was about something else.
#
# Every package in `go list ./...` must appear. The gate refuses a package it
# has no floor for rather than passing it silently: an ungated package is
# indistinguishable from a package at 0 %, which is the hole this file exists to
# close.
#
# Format: <package path relative to the module root> <floor percent>
audit 60
auth/basic 84
auth/jwt 88
auth/oidc 92
auth/totp 84
auth/webauthn 83
middleware 67
provider 90
session 86
storage 95

# The examples are runnable demo binaries with no test files, so their coverage
# is 0 by construction and a floor above 0 would be unachievable without testing
# `main`. They are not untested code paths in the library: the compile gate that
# guards them is the build-examples job, which builds and vets them on every
# run. They are listed explicitly because "no floor recorded" is a hard failure.
examples/basic 0
examples/jwt 0
examples/oidc 0
